{"author":{"id":"1949ffc23f11a8cb4f8c42a45c9ad241b49570df9a18687489e9ea78d87c54a3"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1779121665,"nonce":"Q1oWC6JazU55GeFDPDq435OWTBA=","title":"Studio: interaction-first Train/Evaluate collection UI","message":"## Problem\n\nThe seeded RLRMP movement-ramp example made the current Studio pipeline UI failure mode concrete: Train and Evaluate are metadata-first. They repeat stage words, display backend collection/manifests/artifact labels, show hash-like identifiers in primary rows, duplicate the same data in main and side panels, and provide descriptive blocks where the user needs interactive selection, filtering, readiness, and next actions.\n\nThis issue is the first implementation slice after the UX review captured on `0c46f69` and indexed from `93f79f2`.\n\n## Precedents to Carry Forward\n\nThe redesign should borrow interaction patterns, not vocabulary, from these products:\n\n- W\u0026B Projects: project pages separate a run table, workspace, reports, sweeps, and artifacts. The run selector supports visibility, color, search, filter, group, sort, and count; panels can be exported to reports. Source: https://docs.wandb.ai/models/track/project-page\n- Neptune: reports and run selection make visible runs and compared subsets first-class, with report state built from selected runs and chart settings. Sources: https://docs.neptune.ai/reports/ and https://docs.neptune.ai/select_runs/\n- MLflow Tracking: useful baseline for experiments/runs/metrics/artifacts and comparisons, but do not copy its tendency to foreground backend run IDs and artifact paths. Source: https://mlflow.org/docs/latest/ml/tracking/\n- FiftyOne evaluation: evaluation outputs are explorable data views with aggregate metrics and drill-down into examples/failures, not just files. Source: https://docs.voxel51.com/user_guide/evaluation.html\n- Dagster UI: good precedent for readiness, materialization status, run details, logs, re-execution, and provenance without making every raw object primary. Source: https://docs.dagster.io/guides/operate/webserver\n- Prefect work pools: compute should appear as a selectable execution target/channel with defaults and status, not low-level endpoint plumbing by default. Source: https://docs.prefect.io/v3/concepts/work-pools\n- Galaxy dataset collections: many items should be handled as a named collection so batch processing does not become a pile of repeated rows. Source: https://galaxyproject.org/tutorials/collections/\n- Hex/Quarto: analysis products should become curated report/dashboard content while implementation details can remain hidden until needed. Sources: https://learn.hex.tech/docs/share-insights/apps/apps-introduction and https://quarto.org/docs/dashboards/\n\n## Design Intent\n\nPrimary UI should be interaction-centered:\n\n- A visible object should support a user action: select, filter, group, compare, inspect, evaluate, add to analysis/report, trace, or run.\n- Stage identity should not be repeated in headings, pills, and labels. The tab already says Train or Evaluate.\n- The same data should not appear twice. Main content owns collection interaction; the side panel should become selected-item detail/readiness/provenance, or disappear where it has nothing unique to say.\n- Backend nouns (`collection:training-runs`, manifests, raw artifact paths, hash IDs, provider labels) belong in an advanced provenance/details area, not the primary workflow.\n- Training outputs should be summarized by variant name, changed parameters, status, metrics, checkpoint/artifact availability, and downstream actions.\n- Evaluation should be a composer for a named run selection plus protocol/conditions, not a passive input/output count panel.\n- Disabled actions must explain what blocks them.\n\n## First Implementation Slice\n\nBuild a first pass in the web UI, using the seeded RLRMP example as a review fixture:\n\n1. Replace the Train stage main body with a batch/run collection surface.\n   - Show a concise batch summary and readiness strip.\n   - Show the seven RLRMP runs as user-facing rows/cards/table entries.\n   - Include selection, filter/group affordances, key metrics, sweep parameter summaries, checkpoint availability, and an action to create/use an evaluation set.\n   - Remove repeated \"training run\" labels, raw IDs, duplicate manifests, and primary artifact paths.\n\n2. Replace the Evaluate stage main body with an evaluation-set composer.\n   - Use the selected/upstream training runs as the primary input collection.\n   - Make selection interactive with checkboxes and selected-count state.\n   - Show protocol/condition controls and readiness requirements.\n   - Show downstream outputs as meaningful evaluation results, not duplicated backend collections.\n\n3. Convert or suppress the right stage sidebar for Train/Evaluate.\n   - If kept, it should show selected item details, readiness, or provenance collapsed under details.\n   - It must not duplicate the main collection list or expose `collection:*` labels by default.\n\n4. Preserve the existing analysis graph constructor for now, except where required to keep the app compiling.\n\n## Out of Scope\n\n- Real backend execution wiring.\n- Full report-builder redesign.\n- Final analysis-node data binding model.\n- Durable schema changes beyond what is needed for this UI slice.\n- Compatibility adapters for every old project shape; this is allowed to move toward the intended final product model.\n\n## Verification\n\n- Unit tests for any new collection/selection helpers.\n- Existing relevant web tests where available.\n- `npm run build`.\n- Browser review against `http://127.0.0.1:5178/` with the RLRMP movement-ramp example loaded.\n\n---\nCo-Authored-By: Codex (GPT-5) \u003ccodex@openai.com\u003e","files":null}]}